\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 A relational schema organizes data into separate tables, each representing a distinct entity, and defines how those tables connect to one another. This structure is useful for social data because it eliminates redundancy: instead of repeating a candidate's name and party on every single donation row, that information is stored once in the candidates table and referenced by ID elsewhere.\
In the lab's campaign finance database, there are three tables. The candidates table has a primary key 
\fs26 id
\fs24  that uniquely identifies each candidate. The contributors table has its own primary key 
\fs26 id
\fs24  that uniquely identifies each donor. The contributions table sits in the middle and contains two foreign keys: 
\fs26 contributor_id
\fs24 , which references 
\fs26 contributors.id
\fs24 , and 
\fs26 candidate_id
\fs24 , which references 
\fs26 candidates.id
\fs24 . Each row in contributions records a single donation linking one contributor to one candidate.\
Primary keys enforce uniqueness that no two candidates can share the same 
\fs26 id
\fs24 . Foreign keys enforce referential integrity, every 
\fs26 candidate_id
\fs24  in contributions must correspond to an actual row in candidates. Together, they reduce duplication by letting each entity (a person, a candidate) be stored exactly once, while the contributions table records the relationships between them. Joins reconstruct the full picture at query time: for example, joining contributions to candidates on 
\fs26 candidate_id = candidates.id
\fs24  lets us compute total donations by party without ever duplicating candidate-level information across a million contribution rows.\
